PRIVATE REFLECTION: {
Current provisional: O. Symmetrical English Opening (slight edge, ~55%) over G. Sicilian Najdorf (~45%). Round 3 public tally: O 7 (C1,5,7,9,11,13,14), G 7 (C2,3,4,6,8,10,12). L/Grünfeld eliminated. Since previous round, C8 and C12 moved O->G; C11 moved G->O. Earlier notes on C10/C11 movement were partly misrecorded; current round has C10=G, C11=O.

Key O case: actual colors match English/Reversed Sicilian line 1.c4 e5 2.Nc3 Nf6 3.Nf3 Nc6 4.d3 d5 5.cxd5 Nxd5 6.a3, except White knight is still on b1 because 1.c3 was played instead of Nc3. Since the c-pawn disappears after cxd5, 1.c3 is a tempo/development irregularity; White can play Nc3 next. In actual colors there is no Sicilian skeleton: no Black ...c5/...d6, no White d4/e4. a3 is routine prophylactic in English c4/d3 systems; d3 is normal English. cxd5/Nxd5 occurs naturally in the English line, so it is not diagnostic for Grünfeld/Najdorf. O is the only English-family choice, though “Symmetrical English” strictly implies 1.c4 c5; given is really Reversed Sicilian/English.

Key G case: exact color-reversed/rank-reflected mapping to Najdorf tabiya after 1.e4 c5 2.Nf3 d6 3.d4 cxd4 4.Nxd4 Nf6 5.Nc3 a6. Mapping: Black ...e5↔White e4; ...Nf6/...Nxd5↔Nf3/Nxd4; ...Nc6↔Nc3; White d3↔...d6; a3↔...a6; White c3-c4-cxd5↔...c6-...c5-...cxd4, with extra c3/...c6 wasted and invisible after exchange; White Nb1↔Black Nb8. This accounts for missing White Nc3 as Black ...Nc6 in reversed Najdorf. G supporters argue O is less precise because standard English line already has Nc3, and O label normally has ...c5 not ...e5.

Main unresolved dispute: whether “most similar” permits exact color-reversed/reflected match (G) over same-color one-piece-away English/Reversed Sicilian (O); whether O’s non-literal “Symmetrical English” label disqualifies it; whether opening classification normally ignores color-reversed mirrors (Reversed Sicilian is an English, not a Sicilian). Current lean: O, because the actual board is a same-color English/Reversed Sicilian and opening naming usually does not use color reversal, though G’s exact mapping remains a strong alternative. Grünfeld remains eliminated: true Grünfeld needs White d4 and Black ...g6/...Bg7; cxd5/Nxd5 alone is not diagnostic.
}

